L000221	    \end{subfigure}
L000222	    \caption{Detector correlation outcomes for all three measurement bases (H/V, A/D, R/L), used to calculate fidelity of the created (a) $\ket{\Psi^-}$ and (b) $\ket{\Psi^+}$ Bell states, showing successful creation of entangled photon pair with fidelity of 0.82 and 0.70 respectively. The gray dashed lines mark the measurement efficiency for a perfect Bell state. (c) and (d) showing contrast measurement in X-basis as phase is scanned to find the optimum. Solid lines are fitted sin curves of form $A \sin(x+\phi)$.}
L000223	    \label{fig:main result}
L000224	\end{figure*}
L000225	
L000226	A single 854 nm photon is subsequently created by a bi-chromatically driven cavity-assisted Raman transition with a $\sigma^+ + \sigma^-$ polarized laser at 850 nm. Each tone of the bi-chromatic drive is tuned such that both are on Raman resonance with a transition to a different Zeeman sublevel of the D$_{5/2}$ manifold, each of which produces a different polarization of photon, thereby entangling the ion with the emitted photon.
L000227	This ideally produces the maximally entangled state:
L000228	\begin{equation}
L000229	    \ket{\Psi}_{\mathrm{ion-photon}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}(\ket{\mathrm{D}, \sigma^+} - \ket{\mathrm{D}', \sigma^-})
L000230	    \label{eq:ion-photon}
L000231	\end{equation}
L000232	 where $\ket{\mathrm{D}}$ and $\ket{\mathrm{D}'}$ are the atomic states $\mathrm{D}_{5/2},\:m_j=-5/2$ and $\mathrm{D}_{5/2},\:m_j=+3/2$ respectively.
L000233	A second bichromatic cavity assisted Raman transition with 854 nm drive laser maps the state of the ion onto a second photon, hence creating the final entangled photon-photon state:
L000234	\begin{equation}
L000235	    \ket{\Psi}_{\mathrm{photon-photon}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}(\ket{\sigma^-, \sigma^+} + e^{i(\phi+\phi_m)} \ket{\sigma^+, \sigma^-})
L000236	    \label{eq:photon-photon}
L000237	\end{equation}
L000238	where all contributions to the superposition phase from atomic structure and deterministic quantum dynamics are included in $\phi$.
L000239	The additional phase $\phi_m$ is determined by the relative phases of the fields used in the ion-photon entanglement generation stage and the ion-photon state map stage, as the atomic bases in equation \ref{eq:ion-photon} are determined by the entangling fields.
L000240	The two photons have a separation of $6$ $\mu$s with negligible temporal overlap.
L000241	The cavity emission is sent through a motorized quarter and subsequent half waveplate to change the polarization basis of the photons. A Wollaston prism (PBS) is employed to measure the polarization, with each output coupled to a superconducting nanowire single photon detector (SNSPD). The PBS has an extinction ratio of $>$15000:1 for both outputs.
L000242	In order to measure the photon-photon entanglement fidelity, we produce the $\ket{\Psi^{\pm}}$ Bell states, for which the fidelities are given by:
L000243	\begin{equation}
L000244	    \mathcal{F}_{\pm} = \frac{1}{4}(1 \pm \langle XX \rangle \pm \langle YY \rangle - \langle ZZ \rangle),
L000245	    \label{eq:Fidelity}
L000246	\end{equation}
L000247	with $\langle XX \rangle$, $\langle YY \rangle$ and $\langle ZZ \rangle$ being the
L000248	expectation values for the two-photon Pauli operators.
L000249	For the pure state shown in equation \ref{eq:photon-photon}, $\langle XX\rangle$ can be expressed as:
L000250	\begin{equation}
L000251	    \langle XX\rangle=\cos (\phi+\phi_m),
L000252	\end{equation}
L000253	and hence
L000254	\begin{equation}
L000255	   \langle XX\rangle=\begin{cases}
L000256	       +1 \:\;\text{for}\:\; \ket{\Psi^{+}}\\
L000257	       -1 \:\;\text{for}\:\; \ket{\Psi^{-}}.
L000258	   \end{cases}
L000259	\end{equation}
L000260	Thus, by measuring $\langle XX\rangle$ whilst tuning the relative phase between the two drive beams for the second photon generation step, we can tune the superposition phase to the desired target states. During the tuning process, oscillations in value of $\langle XX\rangle$ were observed, demonstrating control of the phase of the entangled state, with the contrast of the oscillations serving as an indication of the degree of coherence of the entanglement.
L000261	
L000262	Based on this, the experimental sequence proceeded as follows:
L000263	first the 850 nm bichromatic drive beam powers and detunings were optimized to generate a photon with polarizations $\sigma^+$ and $\sigma^-$ with equal probability. This was then repeated for the second photon with 854 nm laser. Subsequently the RF phase on one of the tones in the bichromatic drive used for ion-photon state map was tuned whilst measuring $\langle XX\rangle$ to achieve the desired phase, generating $\ket{\Psi^{-}}$ or $\ket{\Psi^{+}}$ states (see Figure \ref{fig:main result}(c-d)). To measure the fidelity, 100 two-photon events were measured per basis. Two-photon events were rejected by post selection based on arrival time to remove any events where a photon was measured outside of its timing window, in order to minimize the effects of detector dark counts.
L000264	
L000265	The measurement results in each basis for the $\ket{\Psi^{-}}$ and $\ket{\Psi^{+}}$ states are shown in Figure \ref{fig:main result}. For $\ket{\Psi^{-}}$, 94 two-photon events contributed to the measurement of $\langle XX\rangle=-0.72 \pm 0.07$, 94 two-photon events to the measurement of $\langle YY\rangle=-0.68 \pm 0.08$ and 92 two-photon events to the measurement of $\langle ZZ\rangle= -0.87 \pm 0.05$ corresponding to a fidelity of $\mathcal{F}= 0.82 \pm0.03$.
L000266	For $\ket{\Psi^{+}}$, 94 two-photon events contributed to the measurement of $\langle XX\rangle=0.70 \pm 0.07$, 92 two-photon events to the measurement of $\langle YY\rangle=0.39 \pm 0.10$ and 93 two-photon to the measurement of $\langle ZZ\rangle= -0.70 \pm 0.07$ corresponding to a fidelity of $\mathcal{F}=0.70 \pm 0.04$.
L000267	
L000268	The main contribution to the infidelities comes from uncertainty in the Zeeman splitting of the two atomic states.
L000269	Any discrepancy between the Zeeman splitting and frequency splitting in the drive fields used for ion-photon entanglement and state mapping leads to an energy difference between the two polarizations of photons.
L000270	A consequence of this energy difference is a phase accumulation between the detection of the two photons at rate $\Delta$, where $\Delta$ is the energy difference between the two photons  \cite{doi:10.1126/science.1143835,stute2012}.
L000271	Using our phase control, we can compensate for the average phase accumulation, but due to the finite size of the photon wave-packet there is a random (near Gaussian) distribution in the separation of arrival times and hence a random distribution of superposition phases.
L000272	If we let $\tau$ represent the difference between the average precession time and the precession time for a given two photon event, this infidelity can be estimated using the analytic formula,
L000273	\begin{equation}
L000274	    \epsilon = 1-\frac{1}{2}\int^\infty_{-\infty} P(\tau)\left(1+\cos(\Delta \tau)\right) d\tau
L000275	\end{equation}
L000276	for probability distribution $P(\tau)$.
L000277	Because we are not post-selecting with respect to the detection time of the two photons (in contrast to \cite{doi:10.1126/science.1143835}), we have to integrate over all possible detection delays. In our experiment we measured the Zeeman splitting using the single photon emission spectrum to better than 200 kHz.
L000278	We fit $P(\tau)$ using the photon arrival times and found that this effect can contribute an error of up to 25\% to for a worst case measurement of the Zeeman splitting.
L000279	
L000280	
L000281	An additional infidelity arises during the state-map procedure. Due to different Clebsch-Gordan coefficients of the two cavity-assisted Raman transitions, an asymmetry in Rabi frequency is required for equal transition probability. This leads to differential AC stark shifts, and hence a time-dependent relative phase accumulation during the generation of the second photon. Simulations of the state map procedure of the full 18-level ion-cavity system suggest that this is accounts for a fidelity drop of $\sim$5\% for optimized process efficiency.
L000282	By decreasing the Rabi frequency of the drive fields, it is possible to improve the fidelity at the expense of efficiency.
L000283	For example, an ion-photon state map with 2/3 optimal efficiency was found to improve the error to just $1\%$.
L000284	In practice, this would require a time intensive experimental calibration.
L000285	
L000286	
L000287	Other contributions to the infidelity budget are magnetic field drift, laser drift and amplitude noise. However simulations indicated that their contributions to the infidelity budget are small ($\sim$1\%).
L000288	
L000289	
L000290	In this publication, we demonstrate the generation of fully phase-controlled two-photon entangled states from an emitter-cavity system for the first time. We have demonstrated the generation of $\ket{\Psi^\pm}$ states with fidelities of above 70\%. The theoretical limit of the fidelity for our experimental parameters is 95\%, limited by the differential AC Stark shift in the photon generation processes due to the asymmetry in our scheme. By decreasing the drive laser intensities fidelities of >99\% are feasible but this results in a 1/3 reduction of the state map efficiency and increased photon pulse length.
L000291	The scheme presented here offers the possibility to generate any arbitrary two-photon state by  interleaving the photon generating steps with a coherent rotation between the two atomic states.
L000292	Full control over the two-photon state's phase enables new schemes in linear-optics quantum computing, quantum memories, and quantum networking.
L000293	
